WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
EPA 608 is not optional. You cannot legally touch refrigerant without it. Let's
make sure you pass.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:40.000
CFCs and HCFCs contain chlorine that destroys the ozone layer. One chlorine atom
can destroy 100,000 ozone molecules. The Montreal Protocol phased out CFC
production (R-12 banned 1996) and is phasing out HCFCs (R-22 production ended

00:00:40.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Here are the key terms. Write these down.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:20.000
CFC: Chlorofluorocarbon. Highest ozone depletion. R-12 is a CFC. Banned.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:40.000
HCFC: Hydrochlorofluorocarbon. Lower ozone depletion. R-22 is an HCFC. Being
phased out.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:00.000
HFC: Hydrofluorocarbon. Zero ozone depletion but high GWP. R-410A is an HFC.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Here's why this matters on the job: EPA 608 exam tests this extensively.
Understanding why regulations exist helps you remember the rules.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Watch out for these: Know the CFC/HCFC/HFC hierarchy. And — R-22 can no longer
be manufactured but existing stocks can be used

00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Quick check: what is the maximum fine for knowingly venting refrigerant? If you
don't know that number, go back and read it again.

